WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:16.173
Mr. Fogg and Aouda set out for a drive through the countryside around Singapore.

2
00:00:16.174 --> 00:00:24.440
Passepartout noticed Fix following them and laughed quietly at the detective's elaborate efforts to keep out of their sight.

3
00:00:24.441 --> 00:00:29.275
While they were gone, Passepartout went about his usual errands.

4
00:00:29.276 --> 00:00:33.237
At eleven o'clock, they were all back on board the Rangoon.

5
00:00:33.238 --> 00:00:36.573
The ship sailed out of Singapore harbor.

6
00:00:36.574 --> 00:00:44.291
In a few hours, the high mountains of Malacca, with their forests inhabited by beautifully furred tigers, were lost to view.

7
00:00:44.292 --> 00:00:49.821
Phileas Fogg hoped they would reach Hong Kong in six days, arriving on November 5.

8
00:00:49.822 --> 00:00:56.263
A steamer for Yokohama left Hong Kong on November 6, and Fogg intended to be on it.

9
00:00:56.264 --> 00:01:01.822
The weather, which had been fine so far, changed soon after they left Singapore.

10
00:01:01.823 --> 00:01:06.749
The sea rolled heavily, and the wind rose almost to a storm.

11
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:16.809
The voyage was rough, but luckily, the wind blew from the southwest, and the steamer sped forward. Passepartout was very impatient.

12
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:22.881
"You are in a great hurry then," Fix said to him one day, "to reach Hong Kong?"

13
00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:26.599
"A very great hurry!" replied Passepartout.

14
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.980
"Mr. Fogg, I suppose, is anxious to catch the steamer for Yokohama?" asked Fix.

15
00:01:32.981 --> 00:01:36.190
"Terribly anxious," said Passepartout.

16
00:01:36.191 --> 00:01:41.467
"You believe he is making a journey around the world then?" asked Fix.

17
00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:45.843
"Absolutely," said Passepartout. "Don't you, Fix?"

18
00:01:45.844 --> 00:01:50.967
"Me?" asked Fix. "I don't believe a word of it."

19
00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:55.912
"You're a sly dog!" said Passepartout, winking at him.

20
00:01:55.913 --> 00:02:04.292
This expression disturbed Fix. "Has the Frenchman guessed my real purpose?" he wondered.

21
00:02:04.293 --> 00:02:09.728
He was puzzled and, after excusing himself, descended to his cabin.

22
00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:15.299
Somehow, Fix realized, the Frenchman had found out that Fix was a detective.

23
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:21.211
From the very beginning of their voyage, Phileas Fogg had been completely indifferent to his surroundings.

24
00:02:21.212 --> 00:02:26.234
Aouda's charm had failed to change Mr. Fogg's behavior one bit.

25
00:02:26.235 --> 00:02:31.330
Every day Passepartout was surprised by Mr. Fogg's cool manner toward Aouda.

26
00:02:31.331 --> 00:02:37.201
It was obvious to Passepartout that Aouda felt a deep gratitude to him for saving her life.

27
00:02:37.202 --> 00:02:42.154
Passepartout was beginning to think his master was quite heartless indeed.

28
00:02:42.155 --> 00:02:49.156
Aouda was also wondering how Mr. Fogg could remain so robot-like, even in her presence.

29
00:02:49.157 --> 00:02:54.345
As the Rangoon approached the end of its journey, the rough weather grew worse.

30
00:02:54.346 --> 00:03:00.113
The wind turned to blow from the northwest, and the steamer fell behind schedule.

31
00:03:00.114 --> 00:03:04.472
On November 3, a tempest tossed the steamer with a fury.

32
00:03:04.473 --> 00:03:09.936
The captain estimated that they would reach Hong Kong twenty hours behind schedule.

33
00:03:09.937 --> 00:03:16.276
Phileas Fogg stood at the ship's rail, gazing at the stormy sea with his usual tranquility.

34
00:03:16.277 --> 00:03:21.352
Aouda was amazed to find him as calm as the day she had first seen him.

35
00:03:21.353 --> 00:03:30.309
His expression didn't change, not for an instant, even though a delay of twenty hours would make him too late to catch the steamer to Yokohama.

36
00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:34.728
If he missed the steamer, he would likely lose his wager.

37
00:03:34.729 --> 00:03:37.523
Fix viewed the storm in a different light.

38
00:03:37.524 --> 00:03:41.633
Any delay would give the warrant more time to reach Hong Kong.

39
00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:48.215
And if Mr. Fogg missed the boat for Yokohama, he would be obliged to remain some days in Hong Kong.

40
00:03:48.216 --> 00:03:53.614
Passepartout, on the other hand, was enraged beyond expression by the difficult weather.

41
00:03:53.615 --> 00:03:56.847
Everything had gone so well until now!

42
00:03:56.848 --> 00:04:01.563
Throughout the worst of the wind, rain, and waves, he remained on deck, working with the crew,

43
00:04:01.564 --> 00:04:07.214
pitching in wherever he could to help the Rangoon's progress through the storm.

44
00:04:07.215 --> 00:04:14.073
On November 4 the sea calmed, the wind turned favorable, and the steamer made good progress again.

45
00:04:14.074 --> 00:04:21.262
Land was sighted on the morning of November 6. They were 24 hours behind schedule.

46
00:04:21.263 --> 00:04:29.267
A harbor captain sailed out to meet the Rangoon, to guide its captain through the channels and islands around the port of Hong Kong.

47
00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:35.836
Mr. Fogg did not hesitate to approach the harbor captain and ask about the steamer to Yokohama.

48
00:04:35.837 --> 00:04:40.787
"The Carnatic leaves at high tide tomorrow morning," said the captain.

49
00:04:40.788 --> 00:04:47.494
"The steamer had to have one of its boilers repaired so its departure was postponed until tomorrow."

50
00:04:47.495 --> 00:04:53.081
"Ah, thank you," said Mr. Fogg, without betraying any astonishment.

51
00:04:53.082 --> 00:04:56.410
At one o'clock the Rangoon was at the quay.

52
00:04:56.411 --> 00:05:02.042
Mr. Fogg led Aouda to the Club Hotel and reserved a room for the young woman.

53
00:05:02.043 --> 00:05:08.396
After seeing that she had everything she needed, he set out in search of her wealthy relative, Jeejeeh.

54
00:05:08.397 --> 00:05:12.808
When Mr. Fogg returned to the hotel, he told her what he had learned.

55
00:05:12.809 --> 00:05:21.942
Mr. Jeejeeh had left China two years earlier. He had retired with his immense fortune and taken up residence in Holland.

56
00:05:21.943 --> 00:05:26.860
In her sweet, soft voice, Aouda asked, "What should I do, Mr. Fogg?"

57
00:05:26.861 --> 00:05:30.975
"It is very simple," he said. "Come to Europe."

58
00:05:30.976 --> 00:05:35.421
Aouda was surprised. "But I cannot intrude—"

59
00:05:35.422 --> 00:05:42.660
"You do not intrude, nor do you delay, my project in the least. Passepartout!" he called.

60
00:05:42.661 --> 00:05:45.154
"Monsieur?" said Passepartout.

61
00:05:45.155 --> 00:05:51.612
"Go to the Carnatic and reserve three cabins."

62
00:05:51.613 --> 00:05:56.675
We have arrived in Hong Kong, and let me tell you, it is a bustling city!

63
00:05:56.676 --> 00:06:03.717
The name "Hong Kong" means "fragrant harbor," and its own harbor was once surrounded by flowering trees.

64
00:06:03.718 --> 00:06:11.143
Hong Kong consists of three main areas─Hong Kong Island, Kowloon Peninsula, and the New Territories.

65
00:06:11.144 --> 00:06:17.045
So Hong Kong is bigger than most people think, with 236 islands in total.

66
00:06:17.046 --> 00:06:23.426
In 1834 Britain attacked China and demanded the country give Hong Kong to Britain.

67
00:06:23.427 --> 00:06:27.704
China refused but did allow Britain to lease Hong Kong.

68
00:06:27.705 --> 00:06:33.165
I find that rather strange, don't you? A country being "rented" to another country?

69
00:06:33.166 --> 00:06:36.941
Maybe you're wondering why the British want Hong Kong for a colony.

70
00:06:36.942 --> 00:06:41.777
Well, it's mostly because Hong Kong is a massive trading port between East and West,

71
00:06:41.778 --> 00:06:50.315
and it acts as the gateway from America to Asia, and from Europe to Asia.
